1. Continuous Integration (CI)

• Pre-flight Checks: Every Pull Request triggers automated code linting, security vulnerability scanning, and isolated test suites via custom GitHub Actions Runners.
• Deterministic Dockerization: Uses multi-stage Docker builds to minimize final image footprints. Assets are compiled cleanly, stripping away development overhead to reduce attack vectors and optimize image sizing.

2. Artifact Management

• Immutable Versioning: Successfully tested builds generate version-tagged, production-ready images appended with the specific GitHub SHA.
• Secure Distribution: Images are programmatically dispatched to Docker Hub utilizing encrypted secrets and scoped access credentials.

3. Continuous Deployment (CD)

• Automated Rollouts: Merges to the main branch orchestrate a secure, SSH-driven payload delivery to AWS EC2 environments.
• Zero-Downtime Pipeline: The runtime layer drops stale dependencies, pulls down fresh immutable image layers, and updates the application footprint safely without manual intervention.

4. Enterprise Observability & Self-Healing

• CloudWatch Agent Synchronizer: An automated management loop triggers during the deployment teardown stage. It validates, configures, and dynamically restarts the localized AWS CloudWatch Agent.
• Log & Metric Ingestion: The engine enforces precise telemetry paths, dynamically mapping application container outputs (stdout/stderr) and OS performance metrics straight to custom CloudWatch Log Groups.

🛠️ Tech Stack & Tooling

• Source & Orchestration: GitHub, GitHub Actions (YAML Workflows)
• Containerization: Docker, Docker Compose, Multi-stage builds
• Registry: Docker Hub (Private/Public Repositories)
• Cloud Provider: Amazon Web Services (AWS EC2, IAM, Security Groups)
• Observability: AWS CloudWatch (Logs, Custom Metrics, Alarms)
• Security & Shell: OpenSSH, GitHub Encrypted Secrets, GNU Bash

🔒 Security & Hardening Features

• Least Privilege Access: GitHub Actions communicates with AWS using dedicated IAM roles equipped with narrow, explicit policies—strictly preventing global root access exploits.
• Secret Isolation: Production credentials, Private SSH Keys, and Docker access tokens are never committed to version control; they are loaded contextually via GitHub Secrets.
• Network Isolation: The EC2 instances reside behind strictly defined AWS Security Groups, opening ports exclusively for necessary secure traffic (SSH on port 22 with limited CIDR blocks, and application-specific proxy ports).
